Task 1: Create an Inventory for Web Servers
Subtask 1.1: Understanding Ansible Inventories
An inventory in Ansible is a file that defines the hosts and groups of hosts upon which commands, modules, and tasks in a playbook operate. Think of it as your contact list for all the servers you want to manage.

Subtask 1.2: Create the Project Directory Structure
First, let's create a proper directory structure for our Ansible project:

# Create the main project directory
mkdir -p ~/ansible-lab13
cd ~/ansible-lab13

# Create subdirectories for organization
mkdir -p inventories group_vars host_vars playbooks
Subtask 1.3: Create a Basic Inventory File
Create your first inventory file for web servers:

# Create the inventory file
nano inventories/webservers.ini
Add the following content to the inventory file:

# Web Servers Inventory
# This inventory defines our web server infrastructure

[webservers]
web1 ansible_host=192.168.1.10 ansible_user=ansible
web2 ansible_host=192.168.1.11 ansible_user=ansible

[webservers:vars]
# Group variables for all web servers
ansible_python_interpreter=/usr/bin/python3
http_port=80
https_port=443
server_role=webserver

[production:children]
webservers

[production:vars]
environment=prod
backup_enabled=true
Key Concepts Explained:

[webservers]: Defines a group named "webservers"
ansible_host: Specifies the actual IP address or hostname
ansible_user: Defines the user account for SSH connections
[webservers:vars]: Sets variables for all hosts in the webservers group
[production:children]: Creates a parent group containing child groups
Subtask 1.4: Create a YAML-based Inventory
Modern Ansible practices favor YAML format. Create an alternative YAML inventory:

# Create YAML inventory
nano inventories/webservers.yml
Add the following YAML content:

---
# YAML-based inventory for web servers
all:
  children:
    webservers:
      hosts:
        web1:
          ansible_host: 192.168.1.10
          ansible_user: ansible
          server_id: web01
          datacenter: east
        web2:
          ansible_host: 192.168.1.11
          ansible_user: ansible
          server_id: web02
          datacenter: west
      vars:
        ansible_python_interpreter: /usr/bin/python3
        http_port: 80
        https_port: 443
        server_role: webserver
    production:
      children:
        webservers:
      vars:
        environment: prod
        backup_enabled: true
        monitoring_enabled: true
Subtask 1.5: Test Inventory Parsing
Verify that Ansible can properly parse your inventory files:

# Test INI format inventory
ansible-inventory -i inventories/webservers.ini --list

# Test YAML format inventory
ansible-inventory -i inventories/webservers.yml --list --yaml

# View specific group information
ansible-inventory -i inventories/webservers.yml --graph
Task 2: Add Machine Credentials (SSH Keys) for System Authentication
Subtask 2.1: Understanding SSH Key Authentication
SSH key authentication is more secure than password authentication. It uses a pair of cryptographic keys:

Private Key: Kept secret on your control machine
Public Key: Copied to target servers you want to access
Subtask 2.2: Generate SSH Key Pairs
Generate a new SSH key pair specifically for Ansible automation:

# Generate SSH key pair
ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096 -f ~/.ssh/ansible_key -C "ansible-automation-key"

# When prompted:
# - Press Enter for no passphrase (for automation purposes)
# - Press Enter again to confirm

# Verify key generation
ls -la ~/.ssh/ansible_key*
Subtask 2.3: Configure SSH Key Permissions
Set proper permissions for security:

# Set correct permissions for private key
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/ansible_key

# Set correct permissions for public key
chmod 644 ~/.ssh/ansible_key.pub

# Display the public key (you'll need this for the next step)
cat ~/.ssh/ansible_key.pub
Subtask 2.4: Distribute Public Keys to Managed Nodes
Copy the public key to your managed nodes. In a real environment, you would do this for each server:

# Copy public key to web1 (replace with actual IP)
ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/ansible_key.pub ansible@192.168.1.10

# Copy public key to web2 (replace with actual IP)
ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/ansible_key.pub ansible@192.168.1.11

# Alternative method if ssh-copy-id is not available:
# cat ~/.ssh/ansible_key.pub | ssh ansible@192.168.1.10 "mkdir -p ~/.ssh && cat >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys"
Subtask 2.5: Configure Ansible to Use SSH Keys
Create an Ansible configuration file to specify SSH key usage:

# Create ansible.cfg in your project directory
nano ansible.cfg
Add the following configuration:

[defaults]
# Inventory file location
inventory = inventories/webservers.yml

# SSH private key file
private_key_file = ~/.ssh/ansible_key

# Disable host key checking for lab environment
host_key_checking = False

# Enable SSH pipelining for better performance
pipelining = True

# Set default remote user
remote_user = ansible

# Timeout settings
timeout = 30

[ssh_connection]
# SSH connection settings
ssh_args = -o ControlMaster=auto -o ControlPersist=60s -o UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null
control_path_dir = ~/.ansible/cp
Subtask 2.6: Create Credential Management Scripts
Create a script to manage SSH credentials:

# Create credentials management script
nano scripts/manage_credentials.sh
Add the following script content:

#!/bin/bash
# Ansible Credentials Management Script

set -e

SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
PROJECT_DIR="$(dirname "$SCRIPT_DIR")"
SSH_KEY_PATH="$HOME/.ssh/ansible_key"

# Function to check if SSH key exists
check_ssh_key() {
    if [[ -f "$SSH_KEY_PATH" ]]; then
        echo "✓ SSH key found at $SSH_KEY_PATH"
        return 0
    else
        echo "✗ SSH key not found at $SSH_KEY_PATH"
        return 1
    fi
}

# Function to test SSH connectivity
test_ssh_connectivity() {
    local host=$1
    local user=$2
    
    echo "Testing SSH connectivity to $user@$host..."
    
    if ssh -i "$SSH_KEY_PATH" -o ConnectTimeout=10 -o BatchMode=yes "$user@$host" "echo 'SSH connection successful'" 2>/dev/null; then
        echo "✓ SSH connection to $host successful"
        return 0
    else
        echo "✗ SSH connection to $host failed"
        return 1
    fi
}

# Function to display key fingerprint
show_key_fingerprint() {
    if check_ssh_key; then
        echo "SSH Key Fingerprint:"
        ssh-keygen -lf "$SSH_KEY_PATH"
        echo ""
        echo "Public Key Content:"
        cat "${SSH_KEY_PATH}.pub"
    fi
}

# Main execution
case "${1:-help}" in
    "check")
        check_ssh_key
        ;;
    "test")
        if [[ -n "$2" && -n "$3" ]]; then
            test_ssh_connectivity "$2" "$3"
        else
            echo "Usage: $0 test <host> <user>"
            echo "Example: $0 test 192.168.1.10 ansible"
        fi
        ;;
    "fingerprint")
        show_key_fingerprint
        ;;
    "help"|*)
        echo "Ansible Credentials Management Script"
        echo "Usage: $0 {check|test|fingerprint|help}"
        echo ""
        echo "Commands:"
        echo "  check       - Check if SSH key exists"
        echo "  test        - Test SSH connectivity to a host"
        echo "  fingerprint - Show SSH key fingerprint and public key"
        echo "  help        - Show this help message"
        ;;
esac
Make the script executable:

chmod +x scripts/manage_credentials.sh
Task 3: Verify Inventory Setup
Subtask 3.1: Test Inventory Connectivity
Test basic connectivity to all hosts in your inventory:

# Test connectivity using ping module
ansible all -m ping

# Test with specific inventory file
ansible all -i inventories/webservers.yml -m ping

# Test specific group
ansible webservers -m ping
Subtask 3.2: Gather System Information
Collect basic system information from your managed nodes:

# Gather facts from all hosts
ansible all -m setup --tree /tmp/facts

# Get specific information
ansible webservers -m setup -a "filter=ansible_distribution*"

# Check disk space
ansible webservers -m shell -a "df -h"

# Check memory usage
ansible webservers -m shell -a "free -m"
Subtask 3.3: Create Inventory Verification Playbook
Create a comprehensive playbook to verify your inventory setup:

# Create verification playbook
nano playbooks/verify_inventory.yml
Add the following playbook content:

---
- name: Verify Inventory Setup and Connectivity
  hosts: all
  gather_facts: yes
  tasks:
    - name: Display host information
      debug:
        msg: |
          Host: {{ inventory_hostname }}
          IP Address: {{ ansible_host }}
          User: {{ ansible_user }}
          Python Interpreter: {{ ansible_python_interpreter }}
          Distribution: {{ ansible_distribution }} {{ ansible_distribution_version }}

    - name: Test file system access
      file:
        path: /tmp/ansible_test
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'

    - name: Create test file with host information
      copy:
        content: |
          Ansible Test File
          Host: {{ inventory_hostname }}
          Date: {{ ansible_date_time.iso8601 }}
          User: {{ ansible_user_id }}
        dest: /tmp/ansible_test/host_info.txt
        mode: '0644'

    - name: Verify sudo access (if applicable)
      command: sudo -n true
      register: sudo_test
      failed_when: false
      changed_when: false

    - name: Display sudo status
      debug:
        msg: "Sudo access: {{ 'Available' if sudo_test.rc == 0 else 'Not available or requires password' }}"

    - name: Test network connectivity
      uri:
        url: "http://www.google.com"
        method: HEAD
        timeout: 10
      register: network_test
      failed_when: false

    - name: Display network status
      debug:
        msg: "Internet connectivity: {{ 'Available' if network_test.status == 200 else 'Limited or unavailable' }}"

    - name: Clean up test files
      file:
        path: /tmp/ansible_test
        state: absent
Subtask 3.4: Run Verification Playbook
Execute the verification playbook:

# Run the verification playbook
ansible-playbook playbooks/verify_inventory.yml

# Run with verbose output for troubleshooting
ansible-playbook playbooks/verify_inventory.yml -v

# Run against specific group
ansible-playbook playbooks/verify_inventory.yml --limit webservers
